<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lietuvininkai</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Prussian Lithuanians</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Lietuvininkai, or Prussian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuanians">Lithuanians</a>, were Lithuanian-speaking Lutherans of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> Minor, the north-eastern part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-prussia">East Prussia</a>. Their region produced the first Lithuanian printed book in 1547 and the poet Kristijonas Donelaitis, and Lithuanian books banned in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> were printed there. Many were bilingual and loyal to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, and after 1945 most of those remaining were expelled or fled.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lietuvininkai.jpg" alt="Lietuvininkai" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was Zscout370 at English Wikipedia. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Lithuania_Minor.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
